---
import Admin from '../../../layouts/Admin.astro';
import Litany from '../../../components/Litany.svelte';
import '../../../styles/atelier/litany.css';
---

<Admin title="Litany" current="litany">
  <div class="litany-docs-overview">
    <p>
      <strong>Litany</strong> is a sample step sequencer. You search for sounds, build
      patterns on a step grid, and shape them with envelopes and effects. Everything
      is encoded in the URL — share the link to send the groove.
    </p>
    <p class="litany-docs-quick">
      <span>Add a voice</span> &rarr; <span>search for a sample</span> &rarr;
      <span>toggle steps</span> &rarr; <span>hit play</span>
    </p>
  </div>

  <Litany client:only="svelte" />

  <div class="litany-docs">
    <details>
      <summary>Voices &amp; samples</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <p>
          Each <strong>voice</strong> is an instrument track. It has a search query
          (e.g. <code>kick</code>, <code>snare</code>, <code>vocal</code>) that
          pulls random samples from the catalog. The voice loads 8 random
          matches into a <strong>pool</strong> and rotates through them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>sample picker</strong> dropdown shows all 8 pool entries — pick
          one to pin it. The <strong>re-roll</strong> button (<code
            >&#8633;</code
          >) refreshes the pool with the same search query so you get new random
          samples without changing the instrument type. The <strong>dice</strong
          > (<code>&#127922;</code>) picks a random instrument type.
        </p>
        <p>
          The <strong>pin</strong> (<code>&#128204;</code>) locks the current
          sample so it never changes, even when the pool rotates.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Step sequencer</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <p>
          The <strong>step grid</strong> controls which beats each voice fires on.
          Click a cell to toggle it — gold means the voice will trigger on that step.
          The number of steps is configurable per voice (1&ndash;32) via the dropdown.
          Different step counts across voices enable polyrhythm.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Euclidean rhythms</strong> are an alternative to manual step programming.
          Click the
          <strong>STEP</strong> button in the controls row to switch to
          <strong>EUCL</strong> mode. You set:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>P</strong> (pulses) &mdash; how many hits to distribute
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>L</strong> (length) &mdash; pattern length (1&ndash;32)
          </li>
          <li><strong>R</strong> (offset) &mdash; rotate the pattern</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The algorithm distributes pulses as evenly as possible. E(3,8) gives
          you the classic tresillo; E(5,16) gives a bossa nova pattern. The
          generated pattern is shown as a read-only step grid below the
          controls. Switch back to <strong>STEP</strong> mode to edit manually.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Play styles</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <p>
          Each voice has a <strong>play style</strong> that controls how samples behave
          when triggered. Open the
          <strong>ENV</strong> panel to change it.
        </p>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Style</th><th>Behavior</th></tr>
          <tr
            ><td><code>one-shot</code></td><td
              >Sample plays to completion. Polyphonic &mdash; overlapping
              triggers are allowed.</td
            ></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td><code>cut</code></td><td
              >Each new step cuts off the previous one. Monophonic &mdash; like
              a hi-hat choke.</td
            ></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td><code>gate</code></td><td
              >Sample stops when the step becomes inactive (playhead moves past
              it). Release envelope is applied.</td
            ></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td><code>legato</code></td><td
              >Like gate, but consecutive active steps don&rsquo;t re-trigger
              &mdash; the sample continues through. Only re-triggers when coming
              from an empty step.</td
            ></tr
          >
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Envelope</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <p>
          The <strong>ENV</strong> panel has an interactive envelope display. Drag
          the <strong>attack</strong> handle (left circle) to set fade-in time, and
          the <strong>release</strong> handle (right circle) to set fade-out time.
          The curve fill shows the shape in real time.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each phase has a <strong>curve type</strong> dropdown:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>lin</strong> &mdash; linear ramp (straight)</li>
          <li><strong>exp</strong> &mdash; exponential curve (eased)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The <strong>preview</strong> button (<code>&#9654;</code>) plays the
          current sample through the voice chain so you can hear the envelope.
          Enable <strong>auto</strong> to preview automatically when you adjust parameters.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>FX</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <p>
          Each voice has a per-voice effects chain: <strong>delay</strong>
          &rarr; <strong>reverb</strong> &rarr; <strong>filter</strong>. Open
          the <strong>FX</strong> panel to adjust.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Delay</strong> &mdash; time, feedback, wet/dry mix</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Reverb</strong> &mdash; wet/dry mix (convolution with a synthetic
            IR)
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Filter</strong> &mdash; lowpass, highpass, bandpass, notch with
            frequency and Q
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Sample rotation</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <p>
          The <strong>rotation</strong> dropdown controls how often the pool advances
          to the next sample:
        </p>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Setting</th><th>Behavior</th></tr>
          <tr
            ><td><code>/HIT</code></td><td
              >New sample every time a step fires</td
            ></tr
          >
          <tr
            ><td><code>/BAR</code></td><td
              >New sample at the start of each loop cycle</td
            ></tr
          >
          <tr><td><code>/4BR</code></td><td>New sample every 4 bars</td></tr>
          <tr
            ><td><code>PIN</code></td><td
              >Always plays the pinned sample &mdash; never rotates</td
            ></tr
          >
        </table>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Sharing &amp; shortcuts</summary>
      <div class="litany-docs-body">
        <p>
          The entire state (voices, patterns, FX, BPM) is compressed and stored
          in the URL hash. Click <strong>SHARE</strong> in the master section to copy
          the link.
        </p>
        <p><strong>Keyboard:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>P</strong> &mdash; play / stop</li>
          <li><strong>&#8984;Z</strong> &mdash; undo</li>
          <li><strong>&#8984;&#8679;Z</strong> &mdash; redo</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</Admin>

<style is:global>
  .litany-docs-overview {
    padding: 12px 16px 0;
    color: var(--lit-text-dim, #7d8590);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .litany-docs-overview strong {
    color: var(--lit-text, #d0d4d8);
  }

  .litany-docs-quick {
    margin-top: 6px;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--lit-text-faint, #5a6270);
  }

  .litany-docs-quick span {
    color: var(--lit-accent, #e6a817);
  }

  .litany-docs {
    padding: 8px 16px 24px;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--lit-text-dim, #7d8590);
  }

  .litany-docs details {
    margin-top: 8px;
  }

  .litany-docs summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    color: var(--lit-text, #d0d4d8);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    padding: 4px 0;
    user-select: none;
  }

  .litany-docs summary:hover {
    color: var(--lit-accent, #e6a817);
  }

  .litany-docs-body {
    padding: 6px 0 4px 8px;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--lit-border, #262c30);
    margin-left: 4px;
  }

  .litany-docs-body p {
    margin: 6px 0;
  }

  .litany-docs-body ul {
    margin: 4px 0;
    padding-left: 16px;
  }

  .litany-docs-body li {
    margin: 3px 0;
  }

  .litany-docs-body code {
    background: var(--lit-cell, #181d21);
    padding: 1px 4px;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
  }

  .litany-docs-body table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 8px 0;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
  }

  .litany-docs-body th,
  .litany-docs-body td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 4px 6px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--lit-border, #262c30);
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  .litany-docs-body th {
    color: var(--lit-text, #d0d4d8);
    font-weight: 600;
    width: 20%;
  }
</style>
